<h2 id="how-to-use">How to Use Your Box</h2>
<p>A plyo box covers more than box jumps. These five drills cover most of what you need for jump training:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Box jump:</strong> 4×5, full rest, step down between reps. Build height for power, not for a highlight clip.</li>
<li><strong>Depth jump:</strong> Step off a 12–18" box, land and jump instantly. 4×4–6. This is the best drill for reactive strength.</li>
<li><strong>Seated box jump:</strong> Start seated on a low box, then jump onto a second box. It removes the countermovement to build starting power.</li>
<li><strong>Rear-foot-elevated split squat:</strong> Use the lowest side. Single-leg strength drives one-foot takeoffs.</li>
<li><strong>Step-up:</strong> A good warm-up or accessory exercise, and safe on any box material.</li>
</ul>
<p>For a full week-by-week plan built around these drills, follow our <a href="/how-to-increase-vertical-jump">8-week vertical jump program</a>. If dunking is the goal, pair it with <a href="/how-much-to-squat-to-dunk">the squat targets you need to dunk</a>. Warm up first with the <a href="/best-resistance-bands-for-athletes">right resistance bands</a>.</p>
<h2 id="mistakes">Common Box Mistakes</h2>
<p><strong>Jumping down after every rep.</strong> Rebounding off a high box adds impact with no training benefit. Step down.</p>
<p><strong>Chasing height.</strong> If you land in a deep squat, lower the box. Measure progress with your vertical, not the box.</p>
<p><strong>Doing box jumps tired.</strong> Plyometrics belong at the start of a session, when your nervous system is fresh, not as conditioning at the end.</p>
<p><strong>Ignoring the floor.</strong> Keep the box on a flat, non-slip surface. Foam boxes can slide on a smooth floor.</p>
<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What height plyo box should I get?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Match the box to your current vertical, not your ego. Athletes jumping under 20 inches should train on 12–16 inch boxes, 20–26 inch jumpers on 16–20 inches, and 26–32 inch jumpers on 20–24 inches. If you land in a deeper squat than you took off from, the box is too high. A 3-in-1 box gives you three heights in one, which covers most athletes for years.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Is a soft or wooden plyo box better?</div>
<div class="faq-a">For most athletes, soft. A foam box lets you push toward your max height without worrying about scraping or cracking a shin on a missed jump, and that confidence matters for jump quality. Wood gives a firmer, more stable takeoff and costs less, which suits experienced jumpers and step-up or split-squat work.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">What size box do you need for depth jumps?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Depth jumps are done by stepping off a box, not jumping onto one, and the drop height should stay low: 12 inches for beginners and up to about 18 inches for trained athletes. Higher drops slow down your ground contact and turn the drill into a landing exercise instead of a reactive-power one.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">Do box jumps actually increase your vertical?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Box jumps build jump power and confidence, but they mostly train how high you can pull your knees up, not how much force you produce. They work best paired with heavy squats and depth jumps. Strength plus reactive plyometrics is what drives real vertical gains.</div>
</div>
<div class="faq-item">
<div class="faq-q">How much weight capacity does a plyo box need?</div>
<div class="faq-a">Look for a capacity well above your bodyweight, especially if you plan to do weighted step-ups or jumps with a vest. Many foam boxes are rated 250–450 lb depending on the model and size, and some compact versions are rated lower, so check the exact listing before you buy.</div>
</div>
